\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS- \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to support crown. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robin Shepherd \par \uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 1999\par 18:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, May 17 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in two waves to support the crown on Monday, buying 30 million euros worth of crowns after a corporate order of a similar amount had pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sharply lower. \par \par \uc2  Slovakia held the first round of presidential elections over the weekend but market players questioned by Reuters said this was not an important issue in the day's events although the conclusion of the final round in two weeks time could be a stabilising factor for the crown in the longer term. \par \par \uc2  "Today's weakening was purely customer driven and nothing to do with the presidential elections. Looking at it longer and broader maybe it could have some effect but this not what we saw today," said Dalibor Cernicka of MMB Brokers in Bratislava. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the bank had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at between 46.7 and 46.3 to the euro. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that its last entry on the market had been at around 46.5. By late afternoon, the crown was being quoted at the same level. \par \par \uc2  Both the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and traders said they believed the initial weakening had been made on a single client order rather than speculation. The underlying reasons for the order were not clear. \par \par \uc2  "It wasn't a speculative deal, it was a corporate order of a relatively big amount and since the market is very thin the exchange rate just weakened...If market participants had big positions it wouldn't have affected the market very much," said Jan Toth, ING Bank's chief economist in Bratislava. \par \par \uc2  Toth said that given the size of the market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have an effect when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  but that it would be ill advised to do so on a continuous basis because of the low level of offical reserves. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  told Reuters earlier that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been in line with its established policy of moving against sharp fluctuations but not in favour of any specific rate. \par \par \uc2  Foreign exhange reserves stood at $2.76 billion as of May 12. \par \par \uc2  Slovak Finance Minister Brigita Schmognerova said on Monday that the strong showing of the government candidate in the first round of presidential elections over the weekend was a good signal for the crown. \par \par \uc2  "The results of the elections are a fairly good signal for the crown not to have to decline as sharply as it has up until now," she told journalists. \par \par \uc2  Some of the weakening of the Slovak crown last week, although not on Monday, had been attributed by dealers to nervousness from abroad about the participation of former premier Vladimir Meciar in the presidential elections. \par \par \uc2  Although Meciar took 37 percent of the vote he will contest the second round against clear favourite Rudolf Schuster who took 47 percent and has the backing of the four party coalition government. ((Bratislava Newsroom, 421-7-5341-8402, fax, 421-7-5341-8403 bratislava.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv5h00xvk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
