\par\par \b \uc2  Slovak prime minister says crown fall not dramatic. \b0\par\par\uc2 296 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 12:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  FRANKFURT, March 11 (Reuters) - Slovak Prime Minister Mikulas Dzurinda said on Thursday that he did not view the recent fall in the Slovak crown as dramatic and added that he saw a level of less than 40 crowns per dollar as optimal. \par \par \uc2  "It is true that the crown has weakened over the last few days but I don't see this as anything dramatic," Dzurinda told a news conference hosted by Deutsche Bank AG . \par \par \uc2  Dzurinda added that the government's plan to slash budget and current account deficits in 1999 should offer the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  support over the longer term. \par \par \uc2  "It seems that it would be optimal if (the crown exchange rate) would be below 40 crowns to the dollar," Dzurinda said. \par \par \uc2  The crown was quoted at 40.72/82 to the dollar at 0820 GMT and 44.40/50 to the euro. It has fallen from a level of about 43 to the dollar over the last two weeks, forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since October's crown flotation. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Brigita Schmognerova said the crown's bid was triggered by a decline in short-term market interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Asked at what level the government would like to see interest rates, Schmognerova said that the 1999 budget plan assumed a further decline in interest rates but she offered no concrete forecasts. \par \par \uc2  The new Slovak government which came to power last October plans to cut the budget deficit to below two percent of gross domestic product and reduce the current account deficit to about five percent of GDP from about 11 percent of GDP last year. ((Tomasz Janowski, Frankfurt Newsroom +49 69 756525, frankfurt.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv3b04f58\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
