\par\par \b \uc2  SLOVAK CROWN EDGES UP AS CZECH CROWN STEADIES. \b0\par\par\uc2 226 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, Dec 2 (Reuters) - The Slovak crown edged up slightly against its mark/dollar basket on Tuesday morning as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil in the neighbouring Czech Republic abated a little. \par \par \uc2  The Slovak crown was trading at 34.604/34.654 to the dollar and at 19.489/19.528 to the mark. The crown is tied to a mark/dollar basket and may deviate from the mid-point of the basket by plus or minus seven percent. \par \par \uc2  At the open it was trading at as low as -2.35 percent but had recovered an hour later to around -2.20 percent from the basket mid-point. \par \par \uc2  "It (the crown) is strengthening a little bit this morning...It is a little bit calmer," said Tibor Fratrik of ING Bank. \par \par \uc2  The Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hit hard on Monday by a wave of nervousness caused by heavy selling of the Czech crown after Czech Prime Minister Vacalv Klaus and his centre-right coalition government resigned at the weekend. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Sovak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which dropped to a low of around -2.5 percent on the depreciation side of the basket from around -1.7 percent on Friday. \par \par \uc2  ((Robin Shepherd, Bratislava Newsroom, 421-7-521-8402, Bratislava.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020040131dtc200zgp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
