\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  crown settles after Czech cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 197 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, May 16 (Reuter) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  crown steadied after sliding in early on Friday with dealers saying Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Prague could boost its \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  counterpart. \par \par \uc2  "It seems there will be no dramatic movement as nothing special is happening on the market at the moment," Dusan Svitek of Slovenska Sporitelna said. \par \par \uc2  In early trading, the crown moved to -0.68 percent against its mark/dollar basket parity after opening at around 55 basis points below the midpoint. It then settled at about -0.63 percent. \par \par \uc2  Svitek said he expected it might move back to around -0.55 percent, which would pre-empt and \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixing will be important to this (development)," he added. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday the NBS fixed the crown/dollar at 32.990, and the crown/mark at 19.542, implying a deviation against the basket of -0.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  Short-term money market dealers saw the fund rates stable at between 19 to 20 percent, with no sharp changes expected further on Friday. \par \par \uc2  -- Peter Javurek, Bratislava Newsroom, 421-7-521-8402 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5g05hhj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
