\par\par \b \uc2  Slovak c.bank says ready to support SKK if needed. \b0\par\par\uc2 353 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, May 15 (Reuter) - The National Bank of Slovakia (NBS) said on Thursday that it was ready to move quickly to support the Slovak crown if the situation gets "out of control". \par \par \uc2  "We are following the Czech development very closely, and the Slovak crown is under control at the moment, but we are ready to quickly react and support it if it gets out of control," NBS Treasury Director Frantisek Szulenyi said. \par \par \uc2  The Slovak crown weakened by some 40 basis points in nervous afternoon trading on Thursday to around -0.70 percent versus its mark/dollar basket parity. \par \par \uc2  The Czech crown plunged about one percent in early Thursday trading before the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market to boost the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Szulenyi would not say at what levels the NBS saw it neccessary to support the Slovak crown, nor would he disclose what tools would the bank use if the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was needed. \par \par \uc2  "There are some signals that we can expect stronger pressure on the Slovak crown in the near future, involving larger volumes than we have seen so far," he said. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated that up to $100 million has left the country so far today as market volatility increased. \par \par \uc2  Traders say foreign players are trading the Czech and Slovak crowns together, effectively implying a depreciation trend on both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said they expected the Slovak crown to weaken further during the day and that it could even test the 1.0 percent level on the depreciation side of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket midpoint. \par \par \uc2  Szulenyi said the NBS so far did not need to directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  crown, but that an indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through its fixing helped the crown stay steady two weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday the NBS fixed the crown some 45 basis points above the market levels, and some dealers said this could be seen as a signal that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not want the crown to fall too much. \par \par \uc2  -- Peter Laca, Bratislava Newsroom, 421-7-521-8402 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5f05nyc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
